# Paths excluded from the distribution tarball produced by release.yml.
# These exist in the template repo for the GAIA team's own workflow but have
# no business on an adopter's machine. Format is tar --exclude-from: one
# literal path per line, comments with `#` are honored.
#
# Anything excluded here is ALSO skipped by `gaia-maintainer release manifest`, so the
# adopter-facing manifest never references files an adopter cannot have.
# Authoritative narrative of what's maintainer-only and why lives in
# `wiki/concepts/Release Workflow.md` (Distribution Boundary section).
#
# Categories below are ordered the same way that page lists them.

# --- 1. Maintainer-only Claude surface ---
# /gaia-release cuts releases of the GAIA template itself; adopters never
# release GAIA. The release-notes skill translates GAIA's CHANGELOG into
# public release notes for the marketing site, a release-time maintainer
# task, so it ships nowhere just like /gaia-release. /health-audit is the
# maintainer-only autonomous audit + auto-heal loop (runbook lives under
# .gaia/cli/health/, already excluded wholesale via category 10). Other
# /gaia-* commands (plan, handoff, pickup, audit) are adopter-useful and
# must NOT be added here. preferred-base.sh is the maintainer's personal
# statusline left-side renderer; adopters get the right-side-only
# gaia-statusline.sh wrapper which delegates left-side rendering to the
# user's own global statusline. The three code-audit-maintainer-* agents are
# the Code Audit Team's maintainer-only members (framework shell + framework
# TypeScript + skills-prose lenses); adopters audit only their own app via
# code-audit-frontend. /distribution-audit is the maintainer's
# ship-or-withhold audit over newly-shipping files; it drives the
# maintainer-only release CLI and has no meaning on an adopter clone.
.claude/commands/gaia-release.md
.claude/commands/health-audit.md
.claude/skills/release-notes
.gaia/statusline/preferred-base.sh
.claude/agents/code-audit-maintainer-shell.md
.claude/agents/code-audit-maintainer-node.md
.claude/commands/distribution-audit.md
# The re-spawn measurement instrument is maintainer-only: the shared ledger
# lib it writes through, the session-start prune that bounds the ledger, and
# the attribution query that reads it. An adopter clone never runs it, so it
# never has a ledger for any of the three to act on.
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-lib.sh
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-prune.sh
.gaia/scripts/audit-respawn-report.sh
# verify-required-checks.sh declares the required_status_checks the GAIA team's own
# branch ruleset must enforce (job names like "Audit CI Tests" that only exist
# on maintainer-only, release-excluded workflows); an adopter clone has no such
# ruleset and none of those jobs, so the script has no adopter surface.
.gaia/scripts/verify-required-checks.sh
# This check asserts an invariant true of GAIA's own source and of nothing
# else: that no consumer hand-rolls a main-root derivation in place of the
# shared resolver. An adopter
# does not write GAIA's resolvers, and its runners (the .gaia/scripts/tests/
# bats suite and audit-ci-tests.yml) are already maintainer-only, so today it
# ships a check no adopter can trigger and could not act on. Withholding it
# is also what stops its scan list and sibling-suite citations naming
# .gaia/cli/src/, .gaia/cli/gaia-maintainer and .gaia/scripts/tests/ inside
# an adopter bundle.
.gaia/scripts/check-main-root-derivation.sh
# The git path-quoting lint guards GAIA's own framework shell and its CI
# workflow YAML against an unquoted `diff --name-only` or `ls-files`, either of
# which lets git C-quote a path the caller then parses. Withheld on the reasoning
# .gaia/tests/shell-lint.sh gives for itself -- adopters run GAIA's bash but
# never author it, so a linter over the framework's own shell has no adopter
# surface -- and shell-lint.sh, its only runner, is already release-excluded
# wholesale via the .gaia/tests entry, so shipping it would ship a check nothing
# on an adopter clone invokes. It also sources guard-awk-lib.sh, withheld
# below, so the absent dependency would decide the withhold on its own.
.gaia/scripts/lint-git-path-quoting.sh
# The workflow run-interpolation lint guards GAIA's own CI workflow YAML, its
# composite actions, and the adopter workflow templates it renders CI from,
# against a `${{ }}` expression substituted into a `run:` script body. Withheld
# on the same reasoning as the diff-quoting lint above: shell-lint.sh, its only
# runner, is release-excluded wholesale via the `.gaia/tests` entry, so shipping
# this file would ship a check nothing on an adopter clone invokes. It also
# sources guard-awk-lib.sh, withheld below, so the absent dependency would
# decide the withhold on its own.
#
# Note what is deliberately NOT claimed here, because it is false and would be
# the premise a future maintainer re-litigates this decision from: an adopter
# clone does have a populated scan surface. This file excludes 11 of the 13
# tracked workflows individually rather than excluding the directory, so
# `tests.yml` and `chromatic.yml` ship, and `bundle:adopter` renders further
# workflows into an adopter's `.github/workflows/` from templates the manifest
# ships. The withhold rests on the absent runner, not on an empty surface.
.gaia/scripts/lint-workflow-run-interpolation.sh
# The errexit status-read lint guards GAIA's own framework shell, its CI workflow
# and composite-action YAML, and the adopter workflow templates against a `$?`
# read that `set -e` has already made unreachable. Withheld on the same reasoning
# as the two lints above: shell-lint.sh, its only runner, is release-excluded
# wholesale via the `.gaia/tests` entry, so shipping this file would ship a check
# nothing on an adopter clone invokes. It also sources guard-awk-lib.sh, withheld
# below, so the absent dependency would decide the withhold on its own. An
# adopter clone does have a populated scan surface, and the shipped workflow
# templates render into their CI carrying the class's own exposure, so neither
# withhold rests on an empty one.
.gaia/scripts/lint-errexit-status-read.sh
# The SIGPIPE-reader lint flags a short-circuiting reader -- a `grep` or `rg` told to stop early, in whichever spelling the gate's own header enumerates -- standing downstream of a pipe where `pipefail` is armed, in a tracked shell script or in an Actions `run:` body, the shape whose pipeline status inverts on a match because the reader closes the pipe and the upstream dies of SIGPIPE. Withheld on the same reasoning as the lints above: every one of its runners is release-excluded, so shipping this file would ship a check nothing on an adopter clone invokes. Those runners are shell-lint.sh, excluded by the .gaia/tests entry below, and the sibling bats suite .gaia/scripts/tests/lint-sigpipe-readers.bats via the separate .gaia/scripts/tests entry beside it. It also sources guard-awk-lib.sh, withheld just below, so the absent dependency would decide the withhold on its own. An adopter clone does carry a populated scan surface, so neither withhold rests on an empty one.
.gaia/scripts/lint-sigpipe-readers.sh
# The CLI workspace-floor check reports a security floor declared in a second pnpm workspace root's pnpm-workspace.yaml that the lockfile beside it does not apply. Withheld on the absent SUBJECT, which is stronger than the absent-runner reasoning the lints above rest on: the root it checks is .gaia/cli, and every file constituting that root -- src, package.json, pnpm-lock.yaml, pnpm-workspace.yaml -- is release-excluded in the category below, so an adopter clone has no second workspace root at all and the check would exit 2 on every run rather than answer. Every runner of this check is itself release-excluded too, so the withhold holds on either ground alone. The runner set is deliberately not enumerated here: `git grep -l check-cli-workspace-floors` lists every file that references the check, which is the set the runners are read out of, and nothing holds a hand-kept list to the tree, which is how the enumeration that stood here went stale. Nothing about this reaches an adopter's own dependency hygiene: their single root is audited by the shipped `/update-deps` skill and the CI audit template exactly as before.
.gaia/scripts/check-cli-workspace-floors.sh
# The awk-source library the bats-arming shell guards source; every one of those guards is itself withheld above, so nothing on an adopter clone sources it.
.gaia/scripts/guard-awk-lib.sh
# The GAIA_AWK interpreter resolver, sourced by guard-awk-lib.sh just above. Withheld on the absent consumer, a stronger ground than the absent-runner reasoning most of the lints above rest on: every file that sources this library, through guard-awk-lib.sh, is itself withheld, so an adopter clone has nothing that would ever source it. What is NOT claimed: the resolver is generic over any awk call site in the tree, not only the surface it currently serves, so shipping it later to widen GAIA_AWK's governed surface would be a re-decision rather than a correction. The consumer set is not counted here, for the reason the check-cli-workspace-floors entry above gives about its own runner set: `git grep -l guard-awk-lib` reads it out of the tree, and a hand-kept cardinal beside it is a second copy that nothing holds to the tree.
.gaia/scripts/awk-interp-lib.sh
# The shipped-tree leak assertion: it proves no release-excluded path survived
# into the tree that becomes the tarball. Withheld on the absent caller: its one
# caller is release.yml (category 9), and an adopter clone never stages a GAIA
# release, so nothing there would invoke it. Its blocking runner, the sibling
# bats suite .gaia/scripts/tests/assert-no-release-leak.bats, is
# release-excluded by the separate .gaia/scripts/tests entry in category 3.
# What is NOT claimed: the script takes a staging directory and a pattern file
# as arguments and is generic over both, so shipping it later would be a
# re-decision rather than a correction. There is a second, narrower reason to
# withhold it: its subject is the maintainer-only distribution boundary itself,
# which is a GAIA-internal concern an adopter has no counterpart to.
.gaia/scripts/assert-no-release-leak.sh
# Deterministic lint over GAIA's own hook layer; every runner (shell-lint.sh and its sibling bats suite) is release-excluded, so an adopter clone would carry a check nothing invokes. The repaired hooks themselves ship.
.gaia/scripts/lint-hook-cwd-relative-loads.sh
# Deterministic lint over GAIA's own hook layer, withheld on the same terms as its two siblings above: every runner is release-excluded, so an adopter clone would carry a check nothing invokes. The shared arm it enforces (.claude/hooks/lib/jq-availability.sh) and the hooks reaching it both ship.
.gaia/scripts/lint-hook-jq-availability.sh
# The PreToolUse registration read and blocking oracle the two hook-layer gates share. Both consumers are withheld above, so an adopter clone would carry a library nothing sources.
.gaia/scripts/hook-registration-lib.sh

